-- title:
--   Phase space $\mathbb R^{2n}$, the symplectic form $\omega$ and the Weyl operators $U(v)$
-- statement:
--   This module fixes the objects of Barnes' continuous-variable stabilizer formalism.
--
--   1. **Phase space.** A vector $v=(s,t)=(s_1,\dots,s_n,t_1,\dots,t_n)\in\mathbb R^{2n}$; $s$ is the position-shift part (coefficients of $\hat p_i$) and $t$ the momentum-shift part (coefficients of $\hat q_i$).
--   2. **Standard symplectic form** (p.1, after (3)):
--   $$
--   \omega(v,v')=\sum_{i=1}^n\big(s_i t'_i-s'_i t_i\big).
--   $$
--   3. **Weyl (generalized Pauli) operators.** The paper writes $U(v)=\exp\big(i\pi\sum_i(s_i\hat p_i+t_i\hat q_i)\big)$ (2). Here $U(v)$ acts on a wave function $\psi:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb C$ by
--   $$
--   (U(s,t)\psi)(x)=e^{i\pi(\langle t,x\rangle+\langle s,t\rangle/2)}\,\psi(x+s).
--   $$
--   Thus $X(s)=U(s,0)$ is the position shift $\psi\mapsto\psi(\cdot+s)$ and $Z(t)=U(0,t)$ is multiplication by $e^{i\pi\langle t,x\rangle}$, as in the paper's (11).
--
--   Every theorem of the mission is stated about these three objects.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper's normalization $[\hat q,\hat p]=1$ does not give its commutation relation (3) from (2) under any choice of units, while (3), (7) and all examples use (3). The operator is therefore fixed by its action: in this realization (3) holds exactly as printed, and the symmetric phase $e^{i\pi\langle s,t\rangle/2}$ (the Weyl ordering denoted by the exponential) gives $U(av)U(bv)=U((a+b)v)$. The operators act on **all** functions $\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb C$, since the paper's position basis $|q\rangle$ is not in $L^2$; every identity is pointwise and restricts to $L^2(\mathbb R^n)$. $\mathbb R^{2n}$ is the product $(\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to\mathbb R)\times(\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to\mathbb R)$ and $\omega$ is a Mathlib bilinear form.
-- source:
--   Barnes, Stabilizer Codes for Continuous-variable Quantum Error Correction, arXiv:quant-ph/0405064v1, p.1, eqs. (1)–(3) and the definition of ω after (3); X(s), Z(t) p.1; Z(t) as multiplication, eq. (11), p.2

import Mathlib

namespace CVStabilizer

/-- **Phase space `ℝ²ⁿ`**, Barnes, *Stabilizer Codes for Continuous-variable Quantum Error
Correction*, arXiv:quant-ph/0405064v1, p.1, before (2): "a vector
`v = (s1, . . . , sn, t1, . . . , tn) ∈ R2n`".

**Formalization Note** A vector is the pair `v = (s, t)`: `v.1 = s` is the position-shift part
(the coefficients of `p̂ᵢ` in (2)) and `v.2 = t` the momentum-shift part (the coefficients of
`q̂ᵢ`). It is a real vector space of dimension `2n` through Mathlib's product instances. -/
abbrev PhaseSpace (n : ℕ) := (Fin n → ℝ) × (Fin n → ℝ)

/-- **The standard symplectic form on `ℝ²ⁿ`**, arXiv:quant-ph/0405064v1, p.1, after (3):
`ω(v, v′) = Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ (sᵢ t′ᵢ − s′ᵢ tᵢ)` for `v = (s, t)`, `v′ = (s′, t′)`. -/
def omega (n : ℕ) : LinearMap.BilinForm ℝ (PhaseSpace n) :=
  LinearMap.mk₂ ℝ (fun v v' : PhaseSpace n => ∑ i, (v.1 i * v'.2 i - v'.1 i * v.2 i))
    (by
      intro a b c
      rw [← Finset.sum_add_distrib]
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      simp only [Prod.fst_add, Prod.snd_add, Pi.add_apply]
      ring)
    (by
      intro r a c
      rw [smul_eq_mul, Finset.mul_sum]
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      simp only [Prod.smul_fst, Prod.smul_snd, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      ring)
    (by
      intro a b c
      rw [← Finset.sum_add_distrib]
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      simp only [Prod.fst_add, Prod.snd_add, Pi.add_apply]
      ring)
    (by
      intro r a c
      rw [smul_eq_mul, Finset.mul_sum]
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      simp only [Prod.smul_fst, Prod.smul_snd, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
      ring)

/-- The formula of the symplectic form, arXiv:quant-ph/0405064v1, p.1:
`ω(v, v′) = Σᵢ (sᵢ t′ᵢ − s′ᵢ tᵢ)`. -/
@[simp] theorem omega_apply {n : ℕ} (v v' : PhaseSpace n) :
    omega n v v' = ∑ i, (v.1 i * v'.2 i - v'.1 i * v.2 i) := rfl

/-- **The Weyl (generalized Pauli) operator `U(v)`**, arXiv:quant-ph/0405064v1, p.1, (1)–(3).
The page writes `U(v) = exp(iπ Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ (sᵢ p̂ᵢ + tᵢ q̂ᵢ))` (2), generated by the shifts
`X(t) = e^{iπ t p̂}` and `Z(t) = e^{iπ t q̂}`, "with any two operators commuting up to a phase:
`U(v)U(v′) = e^{iπω(v,v′)}U(v′)U(v)`" (3). Here `U(s, t)` acts on a wave function
`ψ : ℝⁿ → ℂ` by
`(U(s, t)ψ)(x) = e^{iπ(⟨t, x⟩ + ⟨s, t⟩/2)} ψ(x + s)`.

**Formalization Note** This is the concrete Schrödinger-type realization of (2) in which (3)
holds exactly as printed. The paper's normalization "[q̂, p̂] = 1" does not give (3) from (2) under
any choice of units, while (3), (7) and every example of the paper use (3); so the operator is
fixed by its action instead: `X(s) = U(s, 0)` is the position shift `ψ ↦ ψ(· + s)` and
`Z(t) = U(0, t)` is multiplication by `e^{iπ⟨t, x⟩}`, as in the paper's (11). The phase
`e^{iπ⟨s,t⟩/2}` is the symmetric (Weyl) ordering denoted by the exponential (2); it gives
`U(av)U(bv) = U((a + b)v)` ("real-number powers", p.1). The operators act on **all** functions
`ℝⁿ → ℂ` (the paper's position basis `|q⟩` lies outside `L²`); every identity stated about them
is pointwise and restricts to `L²(ℝⁿ)`. -/
noncomputable def weyl {n : ℕ} (v : PhaseSpace n) (ψ : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℂ) :
    (Fin n → ℝ) → ℂ :=
  fun x => Complex.exp (((Real.pi * ((∑ i, v.2 i * x i) + (∑ i, v.1 i * v.2 i) / 2) : ℝ) : ℂ)
    * Complex.I) * ψ (x + v.1)

end CVStabilizer


